var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"This page summarizes the public API exported by CodeEdit.jl. For a guided introduction, see Getting started and Editing code.\n\nThe detailed docstrings for exported names are listed at the end of this page.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Handles","page":"API reference","text":"Handles identify parsed blocks and are the primary objects used for searching and editing.\n\nHandle: create a handle to the block containing a file location or method.\neof_handle: create a handle to the end of a file.\nhandles: collect handles for blocks in files.\nreindex: update existing handles after files changed outside CodeEdit.jl.","title":"Handles"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Searching","page":"API reference","text":"search: search handles, files, stacktraces, or exceptions.\noccursin(handle, trace): test whether a handle's source location occurs in a stacktrace-like object.","title":"Searching"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Edits","page":"API reference","text":"Edits are immutable descriptions of changes. Constructing an edit does not modify the filesystem.\n\nAbstractEdit: abstract supertype for edit values.\nReplace: replace a block.\nDelete: delete a block.\nInsertBefore: insert code before a block.\nInsertAfter: insert code after a block.\nCreateFile: create a new file.\nMoveFile: move or rename a file.\nDeleteFile: delete a file.\nCombine: combine edits into one planned edit.\napply!: apply an edit through an explicit version-control specification.\ndisplayed!: mark an edit as displayed.\n\nThe edit1 * edit2 operator is shorthand for Combine(edit1, edit2). Chaining * appends edits to a combined edit.\n\nCombined edits are planned and validated as a unit. See Editing code and Safety and version control for workflow details and failure modes.","title":"Edits"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Version-control","page":"API reference","text":"Version-control specifications determine how an edit is applied.\n\nVersionControl: describe a git repository and default apply! keyword arguments.\nGitVersionControl: convenience constructor for git-backed editing.\nNoVersionControl: explicitly apply edits without version control.\n\napply!(repo, edit, message) applies an edit, stages affected paths, and creates a git commit with message.\n\napply!(repo, edit; default_message=\"...\") uses a default commit message supplied either in the call or in the VersionControl object.\n\napply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit) applies without git while still requiring a displayed review.\n\nDisplay, printing, and string(edit) store the exact plan that was shown. When require_view=true, apply! replans the edit and rejects it if the current plan differs from the displayed plan.","title":"Version control"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Convenience-functions","page":"API reference","text":"filepath: return the file path for a handle.\nlines: return the line range for a handle.\ndocstring: extract an attached docstring.\nis_valid: test whether a handle or edit is valid.\nstring(handle): return the block text for a handle.\nstring(edit): return the displayed edit plan and mark the edit as displayed.\ndisplay(handle): show a handle header and source block.\ndisplay(edit): show the edit plan and mark the edit as displayed.","title":"Convenience functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Exported-names","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Exported names"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.Handle","page":"API reference","text":"Handle(path, line, pos=1; parse_as=:auto)\nHandle(method)\n\nReference to a parsed source/text block.\n\nThe path-based constructor returns a handle to the block containing (line, pos), or to the next block after that location. The method-based constructor returns a handle to a method definition when source information is available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.Handle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.eof_handle","page":"API reference","text":"Return the EOF handle for a file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.eof_handle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.handles","page":"API reference","text":"handles(path; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\nhandles(paths; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\nhandles(root, pattern; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\n\nReturn handles for all parsed blocks in one file, a collection of files, or files under root matching pattern.\n\nIf includes=true, statically resolvable Julia include(...) paths are followed recursively. parse_as may be :auto, :julia, or :text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.handles"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.reindex","page":"API reference","text":"reindex(path)\nreindex()\n\nReparse cached files and conservatively preserve handles for uniquely matched blocks.\n\nThe path form reindexes one cached file. The zero-argument form reindexes all cached files that still exist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.reindex"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.search","page":"API reference","text":"search(handle_set, needle::AbstractString)\n\nSearch an existing handle collection for blocks containing needle.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(handle_set, needle::Regex)\n\nSearch an existing handle collection for blocks matching needle.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(handle_set, trace)\n\nSearch an existing handle collection for blocks referenced by a stacktrace or backtrace-like object, such as a backtrace from catch_backtrace() or a collection of stack frames.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(path::AbstractString, needle::AbstractString; parse_as=:auto)\n\nSearch all blocks in a file.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(path::AbstractString, needle::Regex; parse_as=:auto)\n\nSearch all blocks in a file.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(paths::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, needle::AbstractString; parse_as=:auto)\n\nSearch all blocks in a collection of files.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(paths::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, needle::Regex; parse_as=:auto)\n\nSearch all blocks in a collection of files.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(root::AbstractString, pattern::AbstractString, needle::AbstractString; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\n\nSearch files under root matching pattern.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(root::AbstractString, pattern::AbstractString, needle::Regex; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\n\nSearch files under root matching pattern.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(repo::VersionControl, needle::AbstractString)\n\nSearch all handles returned by handles(repo).\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(repo::VersionControl, needle::Regex)\n\nSearch all handles returned by handles(repo).\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(repo::VersionControl, trace)\n\nSearch all handles returned by handles(repo) for blocks referenced by a stacktrace or backtrace-like object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.search"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.AbstractEdit","page":"API reference","text":"AbstractEdit\n\nAbstract supertype for all edit values.\n\nEdit objects describe source or filesystem changes that can be displayed, validated, and then applied with apply!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.AbstractEdit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.Replace","page":"API reference","text":"Replace(handle::Handle, code::AbstractString)\n\nEdit that replaces the source block referred to by handle with code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.Replace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.Delete","page":"API reference","text":"Delete(handle::Handle)\n\nEdit that deletes the source block referred to by handle.\n\nDeleting an EOF handle has no effect.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.Delete"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.InsertBefore","page":"API reference","text":"InsertBefore(handle::Handle, code::AbstractString)\n\nEdit that inserts code immediately before the source block referred to by handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.InsertBefore"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.InsertAfter","page":"API reference","text":"InsertAfter(handle::Handle, code::AbstractString)\n\nEdit that inserts code immediately after the source block referred to by handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.InsertAfter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.CreateFile","page":"API reference","text":"CreateFile(path::AbstractString, code::AbstractString; parse_as::Symbol=:auto)\n\nEdit that creates a new file at path containing code.\n\nparse_as may be :auto, :julia, or :text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.CreateFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.MoveFile","page":"API reference","text":"MoveFile(old_path::AbstractString, new_path::AbstractString)\n\nEdit that moves or renames a file from old_path to new_path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.MoveFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.DeleteFile","page":"API reference","text":"DeleteFile(path::AbstractString)\n\nEdit that deletes the file at path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.DeleteFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.Combine","page":"API reference","text":"Combine(edits::AbstractEdit...)\nCombine(edits::AbstractVector{<:AbstractEdit})\n\nEdit that combines multiple edits into one planned operation.\n\nCombined edits are interpreted in order and validated as a unit. Applying a combined edit that touches multiple files is best-effort at the filesystem level, so a later filesystem failure can leave earlier operations applied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.Combine"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.apply!","page":"API reference","text":"apply!(vc::VersionControl, edit::AbstractEdit)\n\nApplies an edit using the provided version control schema.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.apply!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.displayed!","page":"API reference","text":"Set or clear the displayed marker for an edit.\n\nWhen displayed is true, this compiles and validates the current edit plan and stores its fingerprint as the plan approved for application. It does not print the diff. Use this only when intentionally bypassing visible review.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.displayed!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.VersionControl","page":"API reference","text":"Version-control specification used by apply!.\n\nvc_type is typically Val(:git) or Val(:none). kwargs stores default keyword arguments for later apply! calls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.VersionControl"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.GitVersionControl","page":"API reference","text":"A git version control specification.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.GitVersionControl"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.NoVersionControl","page":"API reference","text":"A \"version control\" specification that uses no version control.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.NoVersionControl"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.filepath","page":"API reference","text":"Return the path associated with a handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.filepath"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.lines","page":"API reference","text":"Return the line range associated with a handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.lines"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.docstring","page":"API reference","text":"Return a handle's docstring text, if available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.docstring"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.is_valid","page":"API reference","text":"is_valid(handle)\nis_valid(edit)\n\nReturn whether a handle currently refers to a valid block, or whether an edit can be applied without validation errors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.is_valid"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Blocks-and-handles","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"CodeEdit.jl edits files by first splitting them into source or text blocks. A Handle is a stable reference to one such block and is the object passed to search and edit operations.\n\nEdits built from handles can be applied through git or through an explicit no-version-control specification.","title":"Blocks and handles"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Blocks","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"For Julia files, blocks are top-level syntactic units such as functions, types, macros, constants, assignments, imports, exports, and includes. Attached docstrings are kept with the block they document.\n\nFor example, CodeEdit.jl sees a file like this as several separate blocks:\n\nmodule Inventory              # block\n\nconst DEFAULT_TAX = 0.25       # block\n\nfunction price_with_tax(x)     # block\n    return x * (1 + DEFAULT_TAX)\nend\n\nend                           # block\n                              # EOF block\n\nA Julia module is not treated as one large block. The module ... line and its matching end line are separate blocks, while the module body is subdivided normally.\n\nAt the end of each file, CodeEdit.jl creates a special EOF block. EOF handles are useful when inserting code at the end of a file.\n\nFor non-Julia files, blocks are paragraphs separated by blank lines.","title":"Blocks"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Handles","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"A Handle points to one parsed block. It is the object passed to search, display, and edit operations.\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/concepts.jl\", 2)\n# examples/concepts.jl 1 - 3:\nfunction foo(x)\n    return x + 1\nend\n\nBecause line 2 is inside foo, the handle refers to the whole foo block.\n\nHandles are interned for a parsed file: requesting the same block again returns the same handle object.\n\njulia> h === Handle(\"examples/concepts.jl\", 1)\ntrue","title":"Handles"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Julia-and-text-parsing","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"By default, .jl files are parsed as Julia source and other files are parsed as text. Use parse_as=:julia or parse_as=:text to override this behavior when constructing or collecting handles.\n\njulia> handles(\"examples/concepts-notes.txt\"; parse_as=:text)\n3 handles\n# examples/concepts-notes.txt:\n  1 - 1: First paragraph.\n  3 - 3: Second paragraph.\n  EOF:\n\nA cached file has one parse mode at a time. Reloading the same file with a different parse mode invalidates existing handles for that file.","title":"Julia and text parsing"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Handle-validity","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"Edits update handles when their referenced block can still be matched after the edit. Handles are invalidated when their block is deleted or can no longer be matched unambiguously.\n\nUse is_valid to test whether a handle still refers to a valid block.\n\njulia> is_valid(h)\ntrue\n\nFiles modified outside CodeEdit.jl are reparsed automatically when a cached timestamp changes. Call reindex to update cached handles explicitly.","title":"Handle validity"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Editing-code","page":"Editing code","text":"Editing in CodeEdit.jl separates description from execution: first construct an edit value, then choose how to apply it.\n\nHandle -> Edit -> Displayed plan -> Apply -> Commit\n\nEdits are values that subtype AbstractEdit. Constructing an edit does not modify files; it only describes an intended change to one or more handles or paths.\n\nThe standard workflow uses VersionControl to apply the edit, stage the affected paths, and create a git commit. If require_view=true, displaying, printing, or stringifying an edit records the exact plan that was shown. apply! replans the edit and refuses to apply it if the current plan differs from the displayed plan.\n\nIn doctest examples, omitting the semicolon from the edit = ... line displays the edit and marks it as displayed. Calling display(edit) has the same effect.\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\nGitVersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\n","title":"Editing code"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Choosing-an-edit-operation","page":"Editing code","text":"Most edits correspond to one of the following operations:\n\nreplace an existing block with Replace;\ninsert code near an existing block with InsertBefore or InsertAfter;\ninsert code at the end of a file with eof_handle and InsertBefore;\ndelete a block with Delete;\ncreate, move, or delete whole files;\ngroup related edits with Combine.\n\nThe sections below follow that progression.","title":"Choosing an edit operation"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Replacing-a-block","page":"Editing code","text":"A replacement edit changes exactly the block referenced by a handle. This is usually the safest way to update a function, because the planned diff is limited to the selected block.\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/ProjectCode.jl\", 6)\n# examples/ProjectCode.jl 5 - 7:\nfunction foo(x)\n    return x + 1\nend\n\njulia> new_code = replace(string(h), \"x + 1\" => \"x + 2\");\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, new_code)\nEdit modifies examples/ProjectCode.jl:\n6c6\n<     return x + 1\n---\n>     return x + 2\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Change foo increment\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 6d088c4\n","title":"Replacing a block"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Inserting-code","page":"Editing code","text":"Insertion edits are useful when a nearby block provides a stable anchor point.\n\nInsert before a block:\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/ProjectCode.jl\", 6)\n# examples/ProjectCode.jl 5 - 7:\nfunction foo(x)\n    return x + 2\nend\n\njulia> edit = InsertBefore(h, raw\"\"\"\n       const SCALE = 2\n       \n       \"\"\")\nEdit modifies examples/ProjectCode.jl:\n4c5,6\n---\n> const SCALE = 2\n>\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Add scale constant\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit e19fa34\n\n\nInsert after a block:\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/ProjectCode.jl\", 6)\n# examples/ProjectCode.jl 7 - 9:\nfunction foo(x)\n    return x + 2\nend\n\njulia> edit = InsertAfter(h, raw\"\"\"\n       \n       function bar(x)\n           return foo(x) + SCALE\n       end\n       \"\"\")\nEdit modifies examples/ProjectCode.jl:\n10c11,14\n---\n> function bar(x)\n>     return foo(x) + SCALE\n> end\n>\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Add bar\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit dc8f16a\n\n\nUse raw string literals such as raw\"\"\"...\"\"\" when writing Julia code as strings. They avoid accidental escaping of backslashes and dollar signs.","title":"Inserting code"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Deleting-code","page":"Editing code","text":"julia> h = Handle(\"examples/ProjectCode.jl\", 14)\n# examples/ProjectCode.jl 15 - 17:\nfunction helper(x)\n    return foo(x) * 2\nend\n\njulia> edit = Delete(h)\nEdit modifies examples/ProjectCode.jl:\n15,17c14\n< function helper(x)\n<     return foo(x) * 2\n< end\n---\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Remove obsolete function\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit adb22b6\n\n\nEOF handles are unaffected by Delete.","title":"Deleting code"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Creating,-moving,-and-deleting-files","page":"Editing code","text":"julia> edit = CreateFile(\"examples/generated.jl\", raw\"\"\"\n       function generated_value()\n           return :ok\n       end\n       \"\"\")\nEdit creates examples/generated.jl:\n0c1,3\n---\n> function generated_value()\n>     return :ok\n> end\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Add generated file\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 0024785\n\n\njulia> edit = MoveFile(\"examples/generated.jl\", \"examples/generated-renamed.jl\")\nEdit moves examples/generated.jl -> examples/generated-renamed.jl\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Rename generated file\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit e3ef87c\n\n\njulia> edit = DeleteFile(\"examples/generated-renamed.jl\")\nEdit deletes examples/generated-renamed.jl\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Remove generated file\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 2d7466f\n","title":"Creating, moving, and deleting files"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Combining-edits","page":"Editing code","text":"Use Combine, or the * shorthand, when multiple edits are part of one logical change and should be planned together:\n\njulia> source = Handle(\"examples/ProjectCode.jl\", 10)\n# examples/ProjectCode.jl 11 - 13:\nfunction bar(x)\n    return foo(x) + SCALE\nend\n\njulia> destination = eof_handle(\"examples/notes.txt\")\n# examples/notes.txt EOF:\n\njulia> edit = Combine(\n           InsertBefore(destination, \"\\nMoved helper source:\\n\\n\" * string(source)),\n           Delete(source),\n       )\nEdit modifies examples/ProjectCode.jl:\n11,13c10\n< function bar(x)\n<     return foo(x) + SCALE\n< end\n---\nEdit modifies examples/notes.txt:\n3c4,9\n---\n>\n> Moved helper source:\n>\n> function bar(x)\n>     return foo(x) + SCALE\n> end\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Move helper source to notes\")\nApplied: 2 files changed, commit ae34f02\n\n\nEquivalent shorthand:\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/ProjectCode.jl\", 6)\n# examples/ProjectCode.jl 7 - 9:\nfunction foo(x)\n    return x + 2\nend\n\njulia> edit = InsertAfter(h, raw\"\"\"\n       \n       function baz(x)\n           return foo(x) - 1\n       end\n       \"\"\") * InsertBefore(eof_handle(\"examples/notes.txt\"), \"\\nAdded baz to ProjectCode.jl\\n\")\nEdit modifies examples/ProjectCode.jl:\n10c11,14\n---\n> function baz(x)\n>     return foo(x) - 1\n> end\n>\nEdit modifies examples/notes.txt:\n9c10,11\n---\n>\n> Added baz to ProjectCode.jl\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Add baz and update notes\")\nApplied: 2 files changed, commit 18887ed\n\n\nCombined edits are validated after the full combined result is planned. Intermediate states may therefore be invalid Julia syntax, provided the final result is valid.\n\nPlanning and validation are all-or-nothing. Applying a combined edit that touches multiple files is still best-effort at the filesystem level: if a later filesystem operation fails, earlier operations may already have been applied. Use version control so changes can be reviewed and recovered.","title":"Combining edits"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Applying-edits-without-version-control","page":"Editing code","text":"For scratch files, generated files, or other changes that should not create a commit, pass an explicit NoVersionControl specification.\n\nThis mode is explicit so that the call site states that the edit will not be committed by CodeEdit.jl.\n\njulia> write(\"scratch-note.txt\", \"status = old\\n\")\n13\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"scratch-note.txt\", 1; parse_as=:text)\n# scratch-note.txt 1 - 1:\nstatus = old\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, \"status = new\\n\")\nEdit modifies scratch-note.txt:\n1c1\n< status = old\n---\n> status = new\n\njulia> apply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit)\nApplied: 1 file changed\n","title":"Applying edits without version control"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"This chapter introduces the basic CodeEdit.jl workflow: find a block, construct an edit, review the plan, and apply the change deliberately.\n\nThe documentation examples share a small git repository in examples and commit each successful source edit.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Installation","page":"Getting started","text":"Install CodeEdit.jl with Julia's package manager. If the package is not registered, add it from its repository URL:\n\npkg> add https://github.com/perrutquist/CodeEdit.jl","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Loading-the-package","page":"Getting started","text":"julia> using CodeEdit","title":"Loading the package"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Creating-a-handle","page":"Getting started","text":"CodeEdit.jl starts from source locations, but edits operate on blocks rather than on raw line ranges. Use Handle to refer to the block containing a location:\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/MyPackage.jl\", 10)\n# examples/MyPackage.jl 7 - 11:\nfunction foo(x)\n    y = helper(x)\n    z = y * 2\n    return z\nend\n\n\nIf line 10 is inside a function, h refers to the whole function block, not only to that line.\n\njulia> source = string(h)\n\"function foo(x)\\n    y = helper(x)\\n    z = y * 2\\n    return z\\nend\\n\"","title":"Creating a handle"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Listing-handles","page":"Getting started","text":"List all parsed blocks in one file:\n\njulia> hs = handles(\"examples/MyPackage.jl\")\n7 handles\n# examples/MyPackage.jl:\n   1 -  1: module MyPackage\n   3 -  3: include(\"helpers.jl\")\n   5 -  5: const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n   7 - 11: function foo(x); y = helper(x); z = y * …\n  13 - 15: function old_function_name(); return foo…\n  17 - 17: end\n  EOF:\n\nList all blocks in files matching a glob:\n\njulia> hs = handles(\"examples\", \"*.jl\")\n26 handles\n# examples/MyPackage.jl:\n   1 -  1: module MyPackage\n   3 -  3: include(\"helpers.jl\")\n   5 -  5: const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n   7 - 11: function foo(x); y = helper(x); z = y * …\n  13 - 15: function old_function_name(); return foo…\n  17 - 17: end\n  EOF:\n\n# examples/ProjectCode.jl:\n   1 -  1: module ProjectCode\n   3 -  3: const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n   5 -  7: function foo(x); return x + 1; end\n   9 - 11: function helper(x); return foo(x) * 2; e…\n  13 - 15: function obsolete(); return :remove_me; …\n  17 - 17: end\n  EOF:\n\n# examples/concepts.jl:\n  1 - 3: function foo(x); return x + 1; end\n  5 - 7: function bar(x); return foo(x); end\n  EOF:\n\n# examples/error-example.jl:\n  1 - 3: function inner(x); error(\"bad input: $x\"…\n  5 - 7: function outer(x); return inner(x + 1); …\n  EOF:\n\n# examples/foo.jl:\n  1 - 3: function foo(x); x + 1; end\n  EOF:\n\n# examples/helpers.jl:\n  1 - 1: helper(x) = x + 1\n  EOF:\n\n# examples/safety.jl:\n  1 - 1: const SAFETY_VALUE = 1\n  EOF:\n\nFollow Julia include statements recursively:\n\njulia> hs = handles(\"examples/MyPackage.jl\"; includes = true)\n9 handles\n# examples/MyPackage.jl:\n   1 -  1: module MyPackage\n   3 -  3: include(\"helpers.jl\")\n   5 -  5: const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n   7 - 11: function foo(x); y = helper(x); z = y * …\n  13 - 15: function old_function_name(); return foo…\n  17 - 17: end\n  EOF:\n\n# examples/helpers.jl:\n  1 - 1: helper(x) = x + 1\n  EOF:","title":"Listing handles"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Searching-handles","page":"Getting started","text":"Search within a collection of handles:\n\njulia> hs = handles(\"examples\", \"*.jl\");\n\njulia> matches = search(hs, \"old_function_name\");\n\njulia> matches\n1 handle\n# examples/MyPackage.jl:\n  13 - 15: function old_function_name(); return foo…\n\nThe result is a Set of handles. Each matching block can be inspected, displayed, or used as the target of an edit.","title":"Searching handles"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Applying-an-edit-with-git","page":"Getting started","text":"Inspecting handles does not modify files. To change source, construct an edit value and choose how it should be applied.\n\nFor ordinary source changes, use a git-backed version-control specification:\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\nGitVersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\n\nWith require_view=true, displaying the edit records the exact plan. When apply! runs, CodeEdit.jl plans the edit again and refuses to apply it if the current plan differs from the displayed one:\n\nnote: Note\nIn the REPL, evaluating an edit without a trailing semicolon displays it. Calling display(edit) is equivalent.\n\njulia> h = only(search(hs, \"old_function_name\"));\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, replace(string(h), \"old_function_name\" => \"new_function_name\"))\nEdit modifies examples/MyPackage.jl:\n13c13\n< function old_function_name()\n---\n> function new_function_name()\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Rename old_function_name\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 3630f3e\n\nThe edit is written to disk and committed to git. This is the normal CodeEdit.jl workflow: source changes become small, named commits.","title":"Applying an edit with git"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Inserting-at-the-end-of-a-file","page":"Getting started","text":"Use eof_handle when inserting new code at the end of a file:\n\njulia> h = eof_handle(\"examples/helpers.jl\");\n\njulia> edit = InsertBefore(h, raw\"\"\"\n       \n       another_helper(x) = helper(x) * 2\n       \"\"\")\nEdit modifies examples/helpers.jl:\n1c2,3\n---\n>\n> another_helper(x) = helper(x) * 2\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Add another helper\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit c58b1c4","title":"Inserting at the end of a file"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Applying-without-version-control","page":"Getting started","text":"For generated files, scratch files, or other changes that should not create a commit, pass an explicit NoVersionControl specification:\n\njulia> write(\"scratch.txt\", \"temporary = false\\n\");\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"scratch.txt\", 1; parse_as=:text);\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, \"temporary = true\\n\")\nEdit modifies scratch.txt:\n1c1\n< temporary = false\n---\n> temporary = true\n\njulia> apply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit)\nApplied: 1 file changed","title":"Applying without version control"},{"category":"section","location":"searching-errors/#Finding-errors-from-stacktraces","page":"Finding errors from stacktraces","text":"CodeEdit.jl can search for source blocks referenced by a stacktrace. This makes a debugging session into an editing workflow: catch the error, capture the stacktrace with catch_backtrace(), search project source for referenced frames, inspect the matching blocks, then edit the source and commit the fix.","title":"Finding errors from stacktraces"},{"category":"section","location":"searching-errors/#Starting-from-a-stacktrace","page":"Finding errors from stacktraces","text":"Suppose the following call throws an exception:\n\nfunction inner(x)\n    error(\"bad input: $x\")\nend\n\nfunction outer(x)\n    return inner(x + 1)\nend\n\nCapture the stacktrace:\n\njulia> outer(1)\nERROR: bad input: 2\nStacktrace:\n [1] error(s::String)\n   @ Base ./error.jl:44\n [2] inner(x::Int64)\n   @ Main ~/Documents/Julia/CodeEdit/docs/examples/error-example.jl:2\n [3] outer(x::Int64)\n   @ Main ~/Documents/Julia/CodeEdit/docs/examples/error-example.jl:6\n [4] top-level scope\n   @ none:1\n\njulia> trace = try\n           outer(1)\n       catch caught\n           catch_backtrace()\n       end;\n\nCollect source handles and search for frames from the captured stacktrace:\n\njulia> hs = handles(\"examples\", \"*.jl\");\n\njulia> matches = search(hs, trace)\n2 handles\n# examples/error-example.jl:\n  1 - 3: function inner(x); error(\"bad input: $x\"…\n  5 - 7: function outer(x); return inner(x + 1); …\n\nThe result contains handles for blocks whose source locations occur in the stacktrace.","title":"Starting from a stacktrace"},{"category":"section","location":"searching-errors/#At-the-REPL","page":"Finding errors from stacktraces","text":"In a REPL session, capture the thrown stacktrace in a variable such as err and search it in the same way:\n\njulia> err = try\n           outer(1)\n       catch caught\n           catch_backtrace()\n       end;\n\njulia> matches = search(hs, err)\n2 handles\n# examples/error-example.jl:\n  1 - 3: function inner(x); error(\"bad input: $x\"…\n  5 - 7: function outer(x); return inner(x + 1); …","title":"At the REPL"},{"category":"section","location":"searching-errors/#Inspecting-the-most-relevant-block","page":"Finding errors from stacktraces","text":"If the result contains only a few matches, display each block:\n\njulia> for h in matches\n          println(h)\n       end\n# examples/error-example.jl 5 - 7:\nfunction outer(x)\n    return inner(x + 1)\nend\n\n# examples/error-example.jl 1 - 3:\nfunction inner(x)\n    error(\"bad input: $x\")\nend\n\n\nA displayed handle includes the file name and line range, followed by the source block.","title":"Inspecting the most relevant block"},{"category":"section","location":"searching-errors/#Editing-after-locating-the-error","page":"Finding errors from stacktraces","text":"After finding the relevant block, construct a replacement and apply it through git:\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true);\n\njulia> h = only(search(matches, \"error(\"));\n\njulia> fixed = replace(string(h), \"error(\\\"bad input: \\$x\\\")\" => \"throw(ArgumentError(\\\"bad input: \\$x\\\"))\");\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, fixed)\nEdit modifies examples/error-example.jl:\n2c2\n<     error(\"bad input: $x\")\n---\n>     throw(ArgumentError(\"bad input: $x\"))\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Throw ArgumentError for bad input\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 751de5e\n\nAfter a successful edit, existing handles are updated or invalidated as needed. If Revise.jl is loaded, CodeEdit.jl asks Revise to revise loaded definitions.","title":"Editing after locating the error"},{"category":"section","location":"searching-errors/#Searching-included-files","page":"Finding errors from stacktraces","text":"For a package entry point that uses include, start from that file and follow includes recursively:\n\njulia> hs = handles(pathof(CodeEdit); includes = true);\n\njulia> search(hs, \"search\")\n15 handles\n# src/CodeEdit.jl:\n  22 - 22: include(\"search.jl\")\n  31 - 31: export search\n\n# src/search.jl:\n   74 -  88: \"search(handle_set, needle::AbstractStri…\n   90 - 104: \"search(handle_set, needle::Regex) Searc…\n  106 - 121: \"search(handle_set, trace) Search an exi…\n  123 - 130: \"search(path::AbstractString, needle::Ab…\n  132 - 139: \"search(path::AbstractString, needle::Re…\n  141 - 148: \"search(paths::AbstractVector{<:Abstract…\n  150 - 157: \"search(paths::AbstractVector{<:Abstract…\n  159 - 172: \"search(root::AbstractString, pattern::A…\n  174 - 187: \"search(root::AbstractString, pattern::A…\n  189 - 196: \"search(repo::VersionControl, needle::Ab…\n  198 - 205: \"search(repo::VersionControl, needle::Re…\n  207 - 215: \"search(repo::VersionControl, trace) Sea…\n\n# src/spans.jl:\n  116 - 134: \"Return the line range touched by `span`…\n\nRecursive include traversal uses cycle detection, so include loops are visited at most once.","title":"Searching included files"},{"category":"section","location":"#CodeEdit.jl","page":"Home","text":"CodeEdit.jl provides tools for making source edits from Julia. Edits are described as ordinary Julia values, displayed as diffs, and applied explicitly.\n\nRather than editing line ranges directly, CodeEdit.jl operates on parsed source blocks. A Handle identifies a block; an edit such as Replace or InsertAfter describes a change to that block; apply! writes the result through a version-control backend or through NoVersionControl.","title":"CodeEdit.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Manual","page":"Home","text":"Getting started\nBlocks and handles\nEditing code\nSafety and version control\nFinding errors from stacktraces\nAPI reference","title":"Manual"},{"category":"section","location":"#Source-model","page":"Home","text":"CodeEdit.jl represents source locations with Handles. Each handle refers to one parsed block in a file. See Blocks and handles for the exact rules.\n\nFor Julia source files, blocks are top-level syntactic units such as functions, types, macros, constants, assignments, imports, exports, and includes. Attached docstrings are kept with the block they document.\n\nFor non-Julia files, blocks are paragraphs separated by blank lines.","title":"Source model"},{"category":"section","location":"#Basic-workflow","page":"Home","text":"The usual workflow is explicit at each step:\n\nHandle -> Edit -> Displayed plan -> Apply -> Commit\n\nThe following example uses the shared documentation repository, changes one function, applies the edit, and reads the file back from disk.\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\nGitVersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/foo.jl\", 2)\n# examples/foo.jl 1 - 3:\nfunction foo(x)\n    x + 1\nend\n\njulia> replacement = replace(string(h), \"x + 1\" => \"x + 2\");\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, replacement)\nEdit modifies examples/foo.jl:\n2c2\n<     x + 1\n---\n>     x + 2\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Change foo increment\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 90d016a\n\njulia> println(read(\"examples/foo.jl\", String));\nfunction foo(x)\n    x + 2\nend\n\nIf Revise.jl is loaded, CodeEdit.jl asks Revise to revise after a successful edit, so changed method definitions usually take effect immediately.","title":"Basic workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"#Safety-model","page":"Home","text":"Constructing an edit does not modify the filesystem. Displaying an edit shows the planned change. With require_view=true, apply! verifies that the displayed plan is still current before writing any files.\n\nThe standard workflow applies edits through git and records each successful edit as a commit. See Safety and version control for details.","title":"Safety model"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Safety-and-version-control","page":"Safety and version control","text":"CodeEdit.jl separates planning from application. Constructing an edit value does not touch the filesystem; applying an edit does.\n\nDisplaying an edit shows the planned change. Applying the edit replans it, checks that the result is still valid, writes files, and, in the standard workflow, records the change as a git commit.","title":"Safety and version control"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Planning-before-applying","page":"Safety and version control","text":"An edit such as Replace, InsertBefore, or Delete describes an intended change. It can be inspected before it is applied:\n\njulia> handle = Handle(\"examples/safety.jl\", 1)\n# examples/safety.jl 1 - 1:\nconst SAFETY_VALUE = 1\n\njulia> new_source = replace(string(handle), \"1\" => \"2\")\n\"const SAFETY_VALUE = 2\\n\"\n\njulia> edit = Replace(handle, new_source)\nEdit modifies examples/safety.jl:\n1c1\n< const SAFETY_VALUE = 1\n---\n> const SAFETY_VALUE = 2\n\nWhen require_view=true, CodeEdit.jl stores the exact plan that was displayed. Later, apply! plans the edit again and refuses to apply it if the current plan differs from the displayed plan.\n\nThis protects against applying a stale edit after the surrounding file has changed.","title":"Planning before applying"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Git-backed-editing","page":"Safety and version control","text":"The standard workflow uses VersionControl:\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true);\n\njulia> handle = Handle(\"examples/safety.jl\", 1);\n\njulia> edit = Replace(handle, replace(string(handle), \"1\" => \"2\"))\nEdit modifies examples/safety.jl:\n1c1\n< const SAFETY_VALUE = 1\n---\n> const SAFETY_VALUE = 2\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Update safety example\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit fb81aff\n\nA git-backed apply writes the edited files, stages the affected paths, and creates a commit. By default, CodeEdit.jl expects edited files to be tracked by git and rejects creation outside the worktree.\n\nGit is the recommended undo and recovery mechanism. CodeEdit.jl does not provide an undo stack.","title":"Git-backed editing"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Dirty-files","page":"Safety and version control","text":"CodeEdit.jl can reject edits when relevant tracked files are dirty. It can also make a precommit before formatting or applying the edit when precommit_message is supplied.\n\nThis supports two common workflows:\n\nkeep the worktree clean before each edit;\ndeliberately checkpoint existing dirty work before CodeEdit.jl changes anything.","title":"Dirty files"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Applying-without-version-control","page":"Safety and version control","text":"For scratch files, generated files, or temporary changes, use NoVersionControl:\n\njulia> write(\"scratch-safety.txt\", \"temporary = false\\n\");\n\njulia> handle = Handle(\"scratch-safety.txt\", 1; parse_as=:text);\n\njulia> edit = Replace(handle, \"temporary = true\\n\")\nEdit modifies scratch-safety.txt:\n1c1\n< temporary = false\n---\n> temporary = true\n\njulia> apply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit)\nApplied: 1 file changed\n\nThis mode is explicit by design: the call site states that the edit will not be recorded as a git commit.","title":"Applying without version control"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Validation","page":"Safety and version control","text":"Julia files are reparsed before edits are applied. If the final result would introduce syntax errors, the edit is rejected.\n\nCombined edits are planned and validated as a unit, so intermediate states may be invalid as long as the final result is valid.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Combined-edits-and-filesystem-failures","page":"Safety and version control","text":"Combine lets several edits be planned together. Planning and validation are all-or-nothing.\n\nApplying a combined edit that touches multiple files is still best-effort at the filesystem level. If an early file operation succeeds and a later one fails, the filesystem can be left partially changed.\n\nUse git-backed editing for changes that matter, so the result can be reviewed and recovered.","title":"Combined edits and filesystem failures"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Limitations","page":"Safety and version control","text":"CodeEdit.jl is deliberately conservative, but it is not a transactional filesystem and is not a replacement for version control.\n\nIn particular:\n\nmulti-file applies are not atomic at the filesystem level;\nno built-in undo stack is provided;\nhandles can become invalid when their referenced blocks are deleted or can no longer be matched;\nno-version-control edits are not committed unless you commit them yourself.\n\nThe safest workflow is to work in a git repository, require review for edits that matter, and keep each change small.","title":"Limitations"}]
}
